00:01:37.279 --> 00:01:39.785
<v Sherose Badruddin> Welcome
to WordPress Accessibility Day 2026.

00:01:39.785 --> 00:01:42.686
My name is Sherose Badruddin,
and I'm a digital accessibility consultant

00:01:42.686 --> 00:01:44.686
at the University of North Carolina.

00:01:44.956 --> 00:01:47.390
Thank you for joining us
for The Renaissance of Achievement

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:50.701
for People with Disabilities,
presented by Ed Summers.

00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:55.134
Ed is a blind software developer
and accessibility executive.

00:01:55.588 --> 00:01:58.078
His personal mission is to empower
people with disabilities

00:01:58.102 --> 00:02:00.314
to realize their full potential
in the classroom

00:02:00.338 --> 00:02:03.125
and the 21st-century knowledge economy.

00:02:03.701 --> 00:02:06.983
When his hands aren't on a keyboard,
Ed is typically on the go with Loki,

00:02:07.007 --> 00:02:08.442
his trusted seeing eye dog.

00:02:09.025 --> 00:02:12.508
Please feel free to add your questions
in the Zoom Q&amp;A section,

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:14.619
and we'll answer them
at the end of the session.

00:02:14.619 --> 00:02:16.997
Use the chat to connect
with other attendees.

00:02:18.692 --> 00:02:22.916
<v Ed Summers> Hello and welcome, and thank you
for that very kind introduction.

00:02:22.916 --> 00:02:26.080
It is absolutely wonderful
to be here with you today

00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:29.221
at WordPress Accessibility Day.

00:02:29.637 --> 00:02:30.651
It's terrific.

00:02:30.923 --> 00:02:33.504
Now, before I get
started with this presentation,

00:02:33.714 --> 00:02:35.701
I wanna thank everyone out there listening

00:02:36.206 --> 00:02:39.034
for taking the time
out of your busy schedule

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.831
to learn more about accessibility,

00:02:41.855 --> 00:02:43.783
and most importantly,

00:02:43.807 --> 00:02:47.249
thank you for including
accessibility in your craft.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.875
Your individual efforts

00:02:50.374 --> 00:02:51.707
and your collective efforts

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.367
make a difference,

00:02:53.391 --> 00:02:56.505
and we'll touch on that a little
more during this presentation.

00:02:58.724 --> 00:03:02.232
This presentation is about
the renaissance of achievement

00:03:02.256 --> 00:03:03.469
for people with disabilities,

00:03:04.507 --> 00:03:07.408
and it's becoming obvious.

00:03:07.436 --> 00:03:12.416
I'm seeing trends in the disability
community that are making the renaissance…

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.798
It's becoming clear what's happening.

00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:19.895
Let me just go to the next slide here
and tell you about some of these trends.

00:03:20.531 --> 00:03:22.265
Now, this has been
building for a long time

00:03:22.289 --> 00:03:27.143
thanks to the work that you all are doing
to make technology accessible

00:03:27.608 --> 00:03:30.446
and more inclusion and more opportunities
for people with disabilities.

00:03:30.485 --> 00:03:35.152
These trends have been building for
quite some time, for years, for decades.

00:03:35.225 --> 00:03:37.439
But just over the last six months,

00:03:37.463 --> 00:03:40.536
I'm noticing in the disability
community these trends.

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:43.810
Now, as the head of accessibility
at GitHub,

00:03:43.834 --> 00:03:46.653
you know, it's really my job
to keep an eye on

00:03:47.215 --> 00:03:49.434
how people with disabilities
are using technology

00:03:50.093 --> 00:03:52.358
and how technology affects
people with disabilities,

00:03:52.382 --> 00:03:55.364
and also how people with disabilities
are starting to participate

00:03:55.388 --> 00:03:57.678
in the creation of technology,
the building of technology.

00:03:57.702 --> 00:04:01.682
Really what we're trying to do at GitHub
is to empower people with disabilities

00:04:01.706 --> 00:04:03.680
to become builders,
to become innovators,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.654
collaborators at both the enterprise
and open source level.

00:04:08.329 --> 00:04:12.925
I'm seeing fearless experimentation with
technology in the disability community,

00:04:12.925 --> 00:04:16.171
and it's happening—
people who you would not—

00:04:16.195 --> 00:04:20.374
who I would typically not think
would be fearless experimenters

00:04:20.437 --> 00:04:22.936
are starting to really dive
in and roll their sleeves up

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.886
and experiment with tech,
and it's super fun to watch.

00:04:26.871 --> 00:04:31.008
We're seeing more productivity
and more innovation across the board.

00:04:32.286 --> 00:04:35.498
And as a result of all this, there's
more leaders with disabilities,

00:04:35.522 --> 00:04:39.772
people with disabilities that are emerging
as leaders with a very strong voice

00:04:39.796 --> 00:04:44.418
about things that they have to share that
are making positive impact in their lives.

00:04:45.101 --> 00:04:51.460
And perhaps most exciting,
we're seeing just a genuine excitement

00:04:51.484 --> 00:04:53.714
and enthusiasm for the future,

00:04:53.738 --> 00:04:56.375
and it's accessible technology
that's driving that.

00:04:58.758 --> 00:05:01.536
Now, I wanna give you a couple
examples of this just to kick this off,

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.162
and these examples
are coming from this study

00:05:04.186 --> 00:05:08.635
that was published by Anthropic,
back in March of this year,

00:05:08.659 --> 00:05:11.596
and it's called
"What 81,000 People Want from AI."

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.557
There's a link on the screen here
where you can go get it if you need,

00:05:14.581 --> 00:05:16.839
if you want to take a look at the source.

00:05:17.719 --> 00:05:21.025
I wanna give you a quote from a trades
worker in the United States,

00:05:21.817 --> 00:05:25.372
and this person has a… I'm gonna paraphrase
the quote here, which is on the screen,

00:05:25.396 --> 00:05:28.276
but this person has a reading disability,

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:31.009
and they are saying here that

00:05:31.888 --> 00:05:34.705
AI can read past
their reading disorder,

00:05:34.729 --> 00:05:37.096
and it is empowering them to write code,

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.932
to start writing code, and it's
something they couldn't do before.

00:05:39.956 --> 00:05:41.124
Got another quote here.

00:05:42.982 --> 00:05:46.987
This is from a white-collar worker
in Ukraine, and this person's mute,

00:05:47.605 --> 00:05:53.267
and they said that they used Claude
to build a text-to-speech bot.

00:05:53.954 --> 00:05:57.387
And with that little piece of technology
that they built themselves,

00:05:57.411 --> 00:06:00.088
now they're able to have
near real-time conversations

00:06:00.112 --> 00:06:01.515
with people in their community.

00:06:02.455 --> 00:06:03.703
And it's something he says that

00:06:03.727 --> 00:06:05.971
or they say that
it's something they dreamed about

00:06:05.995 --> 00:06:08.974
and didn't think it was possible,
but now with technology, that's possible.

00:06:09.587 --> 00:06:11.011
And they built it themselves.

00:06:11.011 --> 00:06:12.046
Isn't that amazing?

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.139
All right.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.153
Now, I think there are five
drivers of the renaissance.

00:06:18.951 --> 00:06:21.557
All right? And I'll just
enumerate them really fast here.

00:06:21.557 --> 00:06:23.620
We're gonna do a deep dive
on each one of them.

00:06:24.309 --> 00:06:28.894
It starts with driver number one,
which are accessible mainstream AI tools.

00:06:29.867 --> 00:06:33.920
The next one is multimodal
large language models.

00:06:35.068 --> 00:06:38.810
Number three is AI-native interfaces.

00:06:40.104 --> 00:06:43.410
Number four is the democratization
of software development,

00:06:44.202 --> 00:06:45.549
and number five is open source.

00:06:47.046 --> 00:06:48.322
Before we jump into these,

00:06:51.337 --> 00:06:53.670
I need to acknowledge risks of AI.

00:06:54.183 --> 00:06:56.369
These are very real, clear, and present.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:00.691
The one at the top of the list that
perhaps impacts me personally the most

00:07:01.281 --> 00:07:03.213
right now are the biases,

00:07:03.237 --> 00:07:07.296
the bias that's built-in to AI models.

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.697
And to be clear,
that bias comes from the human,

00:07:11.581 --> 00:07:14.141
the human output,
the human content that we've generated.

00:07:14.571 --> 00:07:17.002
All that bias is embedded
in that human content.

00:07:17.972 --> 00:07:21.487
But we do need to remove that bias
from the large language models.

00:07:22.569 --> 00:07:24.788
There's work in progress
to help make that happen.

00:07:25.771 --> 00:07:28.011
Another one that's just
really top of mind right now,

00:07:28.035 --> 00:07:30.628
and has been for the last few months,
as I'm sure you've seen,

00:07:30.652 --> 00:07:33.190
are the risks for cybersecurity.

00:07:34.371 --> 00:07:37.889
And of course, we all know that
there's a need for more data centers

00:07:37.913 --> 00:07:42.080
and more power generation
facilities to drive,

00:07:42.728 --> 00:07:44.585
to satisfy this AI demand.

00:07:44.647 --> 00:07:47.506
And there are environmental
risks that must be managed there.

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:51.085
And then yet another one here
is the potential for rapid change

00:07:51.085 --> 00:07:57.490
in the labor market that basically can be
faster than we can adapt and absorb it.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:01.369
Now, I'm an optimist. I believe
that all these risks can be managed.

00:08:01.369 --> 00:08:05.509
I know for a fact there are smart
people that are engaged on these risks

00:08:05.533 --> 00:08:06.562
and other risks,

00:08:07.056 --> 00:08:11.371
and I'm seeing
a lot of really terrific open discussion

00:08:11.395 --> 00:08:14.876
and debate about how to manage
these risks that's happening

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:16.403
freely and openly in public.

00:08:16.755 --> 00:08:17.973
And all these are good things,

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:22.248
and I'm optimistic that we are
going to be able to manage these risks.

00:08:22.272 --> 00:08:23.582
But I'm not gonna
focus on them anymore.

00:08:23.606 --> 00:08:26.220
I wanna set them aside
for the purposes of this conversation

00:08:26.662 --> 00:08:30.633
because I wanna focus on the benefits,

00:08:30.657 --> 00:08:35.320
the benefits of AI for people with
disabilities for the next 35 minutes or so.

00:08:36.581 --> 00:08:38.285
All right,
let's jump to the first driver,

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:41.431
which is accessible mainstream AI tools.

00:08:43.569 --> 00:08:45.180
I have on the screen on this slide here,

00:08:45.204 --> 00:08:48.971
I have like what I consider to
be the major trends in computing

00:08:48.995 --> 00:08:53.268
that have affected humans and users
over the last 50 year-years or so.

00:08:53.828 --> 00:08:56.711
And it starts
with graphical user interfaces.

00:08:56.713 --> 00:09:00.380
Now, graphical user interfaces were
essentially invented back in the '70s.

00:09:00.611 --> 00:09:04.611
They were adopted,
of course, on Macs during the 1980s,

00:09:04.895 --> 00:09:07.329
and in the early 1990s,
it really went mainstream

00:09:07.353 --> 00:09:11.250
with the integration of GUI,
modern GUI technology into Windows

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.712
and other windowing operating systems.

00:09:14.531 --> 00:09:17.507
And throughout that time, people
with disabilities didn't have equal access

00:09:17.507 --> 00:09:19.812
to GUIs, to the windowing systems.

00:09:19.812 --> 00:09:23.606
And best as I could tell, it really
didn't come online, equal access,

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:27.621
particularly people who are blind, didn't
come out online until maybe the mid-'90s.

00:09:28.187 --> 00:09:30.770
Okay, so we're talking about
from invention to equal access,

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:32.009
we're talking like 20 years.

00:09:32.607 --> 00:09:36.158
Next up, around 1990, the World Wide Web
was invented with web browsers.

00:09:36.158 --> 00:09:39.445
And in the beginning, it was great
because it was primarily text.

00:09:39.445 --> 00:09:43.882
And then we quickly started to add video
and interactive content,

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:46.940
the JavaScript,
the Macromedia, the Adobe Flash.

00:09:47.085 --> 00:09:50.789
And it was, oh goodness,
a good 10 or 15 years later

00:09:50.813 --> 00:09:55.725
that we finally invented the Accessible
Rich Internet Applications or the ARIA API.

00:09:56.341 --> 00:10:02.896
And that became… That passed as a RFC,
as a recommendation at the W3C in 2008.

00:10:03.367 --> 00:10:07.829
And, from then on,
we've still been working to adopt ARIA

00:10:07.829 --> 00:10:12.185
and to include—to make all this rich
web-based interaction accessible.

00:10:12.185 --> 00:10:14.803
We've come a long way
with that, but it took decades.

00:10:14.803 --> 00:10:17.634
Next up, in the 2000s,
touchscreen devices.

00:10:17.634 --> 00:10:19.861
I can remember
when the iPhone first came out,

00:10:19.885 --> 00:10:21.723
it was not accessible
for people with disabilities.

00:10:21.723 --> 00:10:25.951
But with a pretty fast follow-up, VoiceOver
was created, the VoiceOver screen reader,

00:10:25.975 --> 00:10:29.524
which allowed people who are blind
to access those touchscreen devices,

00:10:29.548 --> 00:10:31.758
and of course, there's other
features that have been added

00:10:31.782 --> 00:10:34.710
for people who have mobility disabilities.

00:10:36.286 --> 00:10:38.630
Social media in the 2010s really got big,

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:42.310
and I will tell you today, in 2026, there
are still major social media apps

00:10:42.334 --> 00:10:44.939
that I as a blind person cannot
access with confidence.

00:10:46.389 --> 00:10:48.056
But generative AI is different.

00:10:48.387 --> 00:10:50.036
Almost from the very beginning,

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:52.713
the developers of GitHub Copilot,
and Microsoft Copilot,

00:10:52.737 --> 00:10:56.796
and Claude, and OpenAI,
all these other tools, many… Gemini,

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:00.788
these tools have been designed
and implemented with accessibility in mind.

00:11:00.812 --> 00:11:05.447
And now more than ever, people
with disabilities are getting equal access

00:11:05.471 --> 00:11:10.088
to these tools at the same time
as non-disabled peers.

00:11:10.112 --> 00:11:12.158
It's amazing.
I will tell you the reason for this.

00:11:12.182 --> 00:11:14.783
A big reason for this is
because of your effort.

00:11:14.783 --> 00:11:21.278
Your collective effort has raised
accessibility in the public consciousness,

00:11:21.302 --> 00:11:24.452
and the consciousness
of people who build technologies.

00:11:24.506 --> 00:11:28.229
It's amazing, and it's given us
access like we've never had before.

00:11:28.253 --> 00:11:29.292
Okay?

00:11:29.621 --> 00:11:33.254
Let's go into driver number two,
which is multimodal large language models.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:37.641
Now, this is the definition
of modes on the screen here.

00:11:37.641 --> 00:11:42.229
It includes things like text,
images, audio, video,

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:48.109
and then multimodal behaviors
or multimodal features is the ability

00:11:48.133 --> 00:11:52.454
to convert seamlessly and fluidly
between one or more of these modes.

00:11:54.192 --> 00:11:56.084
For example, text to speech
and speech to text,

00:11:56.108 --> 00:11:58.037
we've been doing that
for quite some time,

00:11:58.091 --> 00:12:02.451
as you know, with things like
Siri on the iPhone and the Amazon devices.

00:12:03.884 --> 00:12:06.464
And then just recently,
large language models…

00:12:06.464 --> 00:12:09.289
In the last few years,
large language models have given us access

00:12:09.313 --> 00:12:12.406
to convert between text to image
and image to text,

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:16.039
which is obviously super powerful for
people who are blind who can't see images.

00:12:16.039 --> 00:12:19.078
We'll touch on that in just a minute,
more in a minute.

00:12:19.624 --> 00:12:20.780
And then really the…

00:12:20.804 --> 00:12:23.673
I think these last two bullets here
are the frontier

00:12:23.697 --> 00:12:26.354
where the really exciting work
is taking place right now,

00:12:26.378 --> 00:12:30.024
and that includes conversion between text
and images, image to text.

00:12:30.035 --> 00:12:31.635
For example… I'm sorry.

00:12:31.635 --> 00:12:33.491
Video to text and text to video,

00:12:34.436 --> 00:12:38.512
where we can get like real-time description
of video feeds for people who are blind.

00:12:38.536 --> 00:12:39.705
It's amazing.

00:12:39.729 --> 00:12:41.488
And then there's a lot
of people working on

00:12:41.512 --> 00:12:44.939
converting sign language to speech or text
and back and forth,

00:12:44.963 --> 00:12:48.586
and including Google just had a really
interesting announcement a few weeks ago,

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:52.028
really nice announcement of
sign language to text features.

00:12:53.589 --> 00:12:56.665
I wanna give you an example
that's really personal to me here,

00:12:56.665 --> 00:13:01.627
which is an app called Be My Eyes and a
feature within that app called Be My AI.

00:13:01.627 --> 00:13:06.631
I have a video here that I'm gonna run,
and let's get started with this video.

00:13:07.909 --> 00:13:15.292
[music]

00:13:15.358 --> 00:13:19.707
<v Hans Jørgen Wiberg (in video)> Be My Eyes is an app
for blind and low vision people

00:13:19.731 --> 00:13:25.400
where you can request a volunteer to see
through your camera on your phone.

00:13:25.694 --> 00:13:28.402
<v Crystal Jones (in video)> It matches people
who are blind or low vision

00:13:28.426 --> 00:13:29.945
with sighted volunteers,

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:33.016
and really sky's the limit
for what you can use it for.

00:13:33.016 --> 00:13:39.306
<v Hans (in video)> We have more than half a million blind
and low vision users across the globe,

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:43.374
and we have 185 different
languages you can choose from.

00:13:43.398 --> 00:13:46.576
<v Crystal (in video)> It really is focused around
how blind and low vision people

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.420
can just be more
independent in their world

00:13:48.444 --> 00:13:50.887
and have access to these visual tools.

00:13:51.042 --> 00:13:54.462
<v Hans (in video)> Our latest feature with Be My AI,

00:13:54.725 --> 00:13:57.869
where you as a blind person take a photo,

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:02.648
and this photo is automatically
uploaded to OpenAI,

00:14:02.672 --> 00:14:06.807
and you will get a detailed
description of the photo back.

00:14:07.046 --> 00:14:08.607
<v Be My AI (in video)> The image you shared
is of a laptop screen

00:14:08.631 --> 00:14:09.514
displaying a presentation.

00:14:09.561 --> 00:14:11.197
The slide on the screen
has a dark blue background

00:14:11.221 --> 00:14:14.582
and contains a graph with a title that
reads, "Technology Drives GDP Growth."

00:14:14.627 --> 00:14:19.268
<v Hans (in video)> We have realized
that a number of our users

00:14:19.292 --> 00:14:21.659
don't use Be My Eyes that much,

00:14:21.683 --> 00:14:22.951
and when we ask them,

00:14:22.975 --> 00:14:27.453
they say, "Ah, I don't feel super
comfortable calling another person."

00:14:27.614 --> 00:14:30.912
So this might be the most
important thing about Be My AI,

00:14:30.936 --> 00:14:33.387
that now you can actually choose

00:14:33.411 --> 00:14:37.356
if you want to talk to
a person or Be My AI.

00:14:37.624 --> 00:14:39.286
<v Crystal (in video)> Some of my favorite
things to do with it

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:41.917
is just get descriptions
of my surroundings.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:43.893
<v Be My AI (in video)> Analyzing picture. Please wait.

00:14:43.917 --> 00:14:45.140
The picture you provided
is of a whiteboard

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:46.555
with various handwritten notes
and diagrams.

00:14:46.579 --> 00:14:48.645
The writing appears to be related to
computer programming or system architecture.

00:14:48.669 --> 00:14:50.324
The handwriting is a bit messy
and some parts are overlapping,

00:14:50.348 --> 00:14:52.804
which makes it look like brainstorming
or working through a complex problem.

00:14:52.812 --> 00:14:56.265
<v Hans (in video)> The fact that
blind people are blind all the time,

00:14:56.289 --> 00:14:58.589
they cannot open their eyes
and kind of sneak peek

00:14:58.613 --> 00:15:01.036
and see what's going on the screen.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:04.041
For the developers,
it was a new territory.

00:15:04.104 --> 00:15:07.390
It's super important that you
actually develop with the people

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:09.116
you are developing for.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:11.898
The app has definitely improved

00:15:11.922 --> 00:15:15.939
from the feedback
we have gotten from our direct users.

00:15:16.305 --> 00:15:21.834
<v Crystal (in video)> It's empowering. I feel like it gives
me the chance to do things on my own,

00:15:21.858 --> 00:15:23.734
to feel a sense of independence,

00:15:23.758 --> 00:15:26.023
to feel a sense
of personal accomplishment.

00:15:26.614 --> 00:15:29.027
<v Ed (in video)> Can you tell me about the desserts,
question mark?

00:15:29.027 --> 00:15:31.512
<v Be My AI (in video)> Matcha ice cream for $24.
Chocolate cake for $18.

00:15:31.512 --> 00:15:33.813
Creamy cake for $16.
Cheesecake for $23.

00:15:33.864 --> 00:15:36.403
<v Hans (in video)> It's not just another photo app.

00:15:36.403 --> 00:15:40.533
It is something that actually
changes people's lives.

00:15:41.016 --> 00:15:48.313
It's our belief that Be My Eyes
will be able to help blind people

00:15:48.337 --> 00:15:52.191
get a job and stay in a job
that they have right now.

00:15:52.539 --> 00:15:56.033
<v Crystal (in video)> We're still just kind
of at the beginning of what's possible,

00:15:56.057 --> 00:15:58.834
and it's gonna be a blast to see
what else they do with it.

00:16:01.819 --> 00:16:05.490
<v Ed> Okay, driver number three is
AI-native interfaces.

00:16:07.302 --> 00:16:10.441
Now, the Model Context Protocol,
or we call it MCP,

00:16:10.956 --> 00:16:15.252
is widely used to create AI-native
interfaces for large language models.

00:16:16.461 --> 00:16:18.772
And really what MCP servers do,

00:16:18.796 --> 00:16:22.432
if you create an MCP server
for your system or for your website

00:16:22.456 --> 00:16:25.944
or for the, you know,
the service behind your application,

00:16:26.275 --> 00:16:29.770
it enables large language models
to connect to those systems

00:16:29.794 --> 00:16:32.775
or databases or services
or whatever they may be,

00:16:33.354 --> 00:16:35.335
and on one side, and on the other side,

00:16:35.359 --> 00:16:38.724
it gives the human
who's using the large language model

00:16:38.724 --> 00:16:44.435
the ability to interact with the system
via the MCP server using natural language.

00:16:45.966 --> 00:16:47.860
And it just opens up
a world of possibilities

00:16:47.884 --> 00:16:50.523
for natural language-based queries
and performing,

00:16:51.497 --> 00:16:56.126
completing tasks with natural language
into these complex systems,

00:16:56.406 --> 00:16:59.330
where in the past, we would
have to do that through an API

00:16:59.366 --> 00:17:02.867
or some kind of a user interface,
like a graphical user interface.

00:17:03.803 --> 00:17:07.688
Now, there's a ton
of benefits for all of us

00:17:07.712 --> 00:17:09.276
when it comes to AI-native interfaces.

00:17:09.276 --> 00:17:10.801
It opens up a world of possibilities,

00:17:11.646 --> 00:17:14.292
but the benefits for people
with disabilities are just—

00:17:14.316 --> 00:17:16.120
warrant some brief discussion here.

00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:19.985
In the disability community, there's
something that we call the disability tax,

00:17:20.458 --> 00:17:25.687
and the disability tax refers to
all of the barriers and extra friction

00:17:25.711 --> 00:17:31.851
that we face as we try to use technology
in order to meet our goals.

00:17:32.768 --> 00:17:40.275
And being able to access new and existing
systems using natural language,

00:17:40.804 --> 00:17:42.839
which is so very universal,

00:17:42.863 --> 00:17:44.273
it's just absolutely incredible,

00:17:44.297 --> 00:17:48.210
and it reduces, by the large degree,
it reduces the disability tax.

00:17:50.079 --> 00:17:54.142
And then if you think about
existing websites

00:17:54.166 --> 00:17:56.526
and the fact that they're designed for…

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:59.865
Well, I mean, frankly,
graphical user interfaces are designed

00:17:59.889 --> 00:18:01.120
for people who can see.

00:18:01.522 --> 00:18:05.822
And they have affordances
for these little visual mechanisms

00:18:05.822 --> 00:18:09.313
that designers use to indicate
things that can be clicked

00:18:09.337 --> 00:18:11.458
and options, operations
that can be performed

00:18:11.482 --> 00:18:15.726
and, you know, suggest what can be done
typically with a mouse, frankly.

00:18:15.726 --> 00:18:17.627
And all those affordances are visual.

00:18:17.627 --> 00:18:22.879
And being able to access the services
and systems

00:18:22.953 --> 00:18:28.056
underneath a website
or an application with natural language,

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:31.292
it really just levels the playing field
when it comes to the affordances

00:18:31.316 --> 00:18:34.922
because we can ask using natural language,

00:18:34.946 --> 00:18:36.478
"Oh, okay, what can I do here?"

00:18:36.989 --> 00:18:39.703
And have a conversation
about that and then go do those things.

00:18:39.703 --> 00:18:42.855
It just reduces the friction all across
the board for people with disabilities.

00:18:44.741 --> 00:18:46.307
Let's move on
to the fourth driver,

00:18:46.331 --> 00:18:50.196
which is the democratization
of software development.

00:18:54.527 --> 00:18:57.777
Hard to believe, just a year
and a half ago, in March of 2025,

00:18:57.801 --> 00:19:02.502
a developer at OpenAI
defined this term vibe coding,

00:19:02.526 --> 00:19:04.538
and his name was Andrej Karpathy.

00:19:04.818 --> 00:19:07.708
Hopefully I pronounced that correctly.
Apologies if I didn't.

00:19:07.708 --> 00:19:11.597
But yeah, but vibe coding,
that term kind of caught on immediately

00:19:11.597 --> 00:19:12.813
because it was a new trend

00:19:12.837 --> 00:19:16.171
where people were basically
building things using natural language.

00:19:17.136 --> 00:19:22.136
And natural language is now today probably
the universal programming language,

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:24.214
and it's really super 'cause
you don't have to learn

00:19:24.714 --> 00:19:28.221
all the kind of pesky semantic details

00:19:28.245 --> 00:19:32.139
of a JavaScript or a C++
or, in a language like that.

00:19:32.163 --> 00:19:34.816
You can express what you want
in natural language,

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:37.444
and it's really opened the door for anyone

00:19:37.468 --> 00:19:40.005
to be able to turn an idea
into working code.

00:19:40.646 --> 00:19:43.154
Now, we all know as
professionals in the field

00:19:43.178 --> 00:19:48.545
that building highly available,
fault-tolerant enterprise-class systems

00:19:48.569 --> 00:19:53.504
requires a new level of,
an additional level of expertise

00:19:53.528 --> 00:19:55.537
beyond expressing what you want.

00:19:55.561 --> 00:19:56.935
Not that it isn't useful there.

00:19:57.067 --> 00:19:59.869
But what I'm seeing that's really super—

00:20:00.405 --> 00:20:03.959
and just kind of points to
the democratization term—

00:20:03.983 --> 00:20:09.814
is that vibe coding is super for
building bespoke personal tools

00:20:09.838 --> 00:20:15.115
or solving like kind of your own personal
access barriers or those kinds of things.

00:20:15.115 --> 00:20:17.678
That's where, I think, it really shines.

00:20:17.678 --> 00:20:20.389
It's like you know,
kind of programming for the masses.

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:24.530
All right. Let's go to number
five, driver number five,

00:20:24.554 --> 00:20:26.286
and this is the last one, is open source.

00:20:30.908 --> 00:20:32.292
Because of open source,

00:20:32.316 --> 00:20:36.592
software development has become
literally the world's largest team sport

00:20:36.616 --> 00:20:38.013
because everyone can join,

00:20:38.577 --> 00:20:41.923
and thanks to so much of the
great work that's been done at GitHub

00:20:41.947 --> 00:20:44.051
on accessibility over
the last four or five years,

00:20:44.458 --> 00:20:48.671
the barriers to participating
have been greatly, greatly removed,

00:20:48.695 --> 00:20:50.783
greatly lowered on
the GitHub platform.

00:20:50.785 --> 00:20:52.821
Thanks to my colleagues at GitHub.

00:20:53.426 --> 00:20:58.068
Open source offers to us as builders

00:20:58.092 --> 00:21:01.536
just a rich collection of tools
and building blocks that we can start with

00:21:01.569 --> 00:21:03.810
that we have a legal right to use.

00:21:03.834 --> 00:21:05.838
And, if you look at the data

00:21:06.357 --> 00:21:08.247
at your typical application
or typical website,

00:21:08.271 --> 00:21:12.261
70 to 90… somewhere between
70 and 90 percent of the source code

00:21:12.285 --> 00:21:14.207
that goes into your typical application,

00:21:14.231 --> 00:21:16.873
enterprise application or website
is open source.

00:21:16.897 --> 00:21:17.964
It's amazing.

00:21:18.576 --> 00:21:21.046
And that's because
there are open-source licenses

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:23.660
that provide a legal framework for us,

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:28.670
for anyone to be able to share our source
code in a way that others can build upon

00:21:28.694 --> 00:21:32.017
and we of course can build
upon the work of others.

00:21:32.017 --> 00:21:34.350
It's a great accelerant to the work.

00:21:35.044 --> 00:21:38.456
And then of course,
thanks to GitHub and platforms like it,

00:21:38.573 --> 00:21:41.845
it provides the online infrastructure

00:21:41.869 --> 00:21:49.435
for this globally distributed collaboration
on technology, on building projects,

00:21:49.435 --> 00:21:51.435
and it's just a beautiful thing.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:55.520
Okay. There is… I've got
a video I wanna show,

00:21:55.544 --> 00:21:58.511
and it highlights the two founders,
the two creators,

00:21:58.535 --> 00:22:02.323
probably one of the most popular
open source accessibility projects,

00:22:02.347 --> 00:22:04.676
assistive technology projects that exist,

00:22:05.025 --> 00:22:06.612
which is NVDA,

00:22:06.636 --> 00:22:10.616
the NonVisual Desktop Access
screen reader,

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:13.471
which I'm sure many of you
are at least familiar with.

00:22:13.852 --> 00:22:14.860
And yeah, I'm gonna…

00:22:14.884 --> 00:22:19.002
we'll run this video for
a short message from the founders of NVDA.

00:22:21.447 --> 00:22:23.789
<v Mick Curran (in video)> I was born with a lot of medical issues.

00:22:23.789 --> 00:22:26.148
Doctors really didn't know
whether I was gonna survive.

00:22:26.148 --> 00:22:30.802
In fact, they made a choice to keep
me alive when I was one day old.

00:22:30.802 --> 00:22:35.451
And so that has been a real
driver that I was given a chance.

00:22:35.451 --> 00:22:40.967
And so therefore, anything that I can do
to make a similar impact for others

00:22:40.991 --> 00:22:43.981
really invigorates me
and gives me meaning.

00:22:44.202 --> 00:22:48.158
Access to technology is a make or break
for blind people.

00:22:48.342 --> 00:22:50.981
In order for a blind
person to use a computer,

00:22:51.122 --> 00:22:54.983
they need special software that reads
all the text on the computer screen.

00:22:55.065 --> 00:22:57.098
We always used to wonder,

00:22:57.122 --> 00:23:00.295
"Why do blind people
have to pay so much money

00:23:00.319 --> 00:23:02.723
when, in fact,
many blind people are unemployed?"

00:23:02.843 --> 00:23:05.068
That idea remained in my mind,

00:23:05.092 --> 00:23:07.012
and I decided to try my hand

00:23:07.012 --> 00:23:10.012
at writing an alternative
to the commercial screen readers.

00:23:10.045 --> 00:23:13.734
<v Jamie Teh (in video)> When Mick approached me with this idea of
writing a completely free screen reader,

00:23:13.758 --> 00:23:16.535
I was intrigued,
but a little bit skeptical, like, look,

00:23:16.559 --> 00:23:19.441
all these companies have been working on
these really expensive products for years.

00:23:19.441 --> 00:23:22.042
What makes us think that we
can write this free thing?

00:23:22.042 --> 00:23:25.096
<v Mick (in video)> In 2006, we started to create NVDA,

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:27.788
which stands for NonVisual Desktop Access,

00:23:28.428 --> 00:23:30.634
which is a free and open
source screen reader

00:23:30.658 --> 00:23:32.657
for the Microsoft
Windows operating system.

00:23:33.046 --> 00:23:35.541
<v Jamie (in video)> We started getting queries
from all around the world.

00:23:35.541 --> 00:23:37.652
Can we translate this into our language?

00:23:37.652 --> 00:23:41.483
And I think that's when we realized
that this had so much more potential

00:23:41.507 --> 00:23:42.736
than we'd imagined.

00:23:43.162 --> 00:23:46.929
A huge part of our philosophy
was how important open source was

00:23:46.953 --> 00:23:50.929
in changing people's lives and everyone
contributing towards a common good.

00:23:51.431 --> 00:23:55.098
We wanted everyone to make a difference
and to empower themselves.

00:23:55.122 --> 00:23:57.636
Part of the development now
is actually done by the community,

00:23:57.660 --> 00:23:59.229
so there's still very much this idea

00:23:59.253 --> 00:24:01.854
that the community shapes
the future of the product.

00:24:01.854 --> 00:24:06.829
<v Mick (in video)> Knowing that there are blind people
around the world doing really cool things,

00:24:07.023 --> 00:24:10.457
running their own successful studios,

00:24:10.481 --> 00:24:12.702
their own self-run businesses,

00:24:12.907 --> 00:24:14.015
it's amazing.

00:24:33.218 --> 00:24:36.301
<v Ed> All right, let's pick this back up
and summarize where we are here.

00:24:38.244 --> 00:24:41.139
There are five drivers
of the renaissance of achievement

00:24:41.139 --> 00:24:42.472
for people with disabilities.

00:24:43.272 --> 00:24:46.189
It starts with accessible
mainstream AI tools.

00:24:46.635 --> 00:24:48.977
People with disabilities
are getting access to these,

00:24:49.678 --> 00:24:53.754
the cutting edge of generative AI
technology through these AI tools.

00:24:55.998 --> 00:24:58.337
We're on the cusp of
really exciting possibilities

00:24:58.361 --> 00:25:04.586
with multimodal large language models that
are converting text to image, to video,

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:05.959
ASL, you name it.

00:25:06.050 --> 00:25:07.745
It's just a very exciting space

00:25:07.769 --> 00:25:09.505
that's opening up all
kinds of possibilities

00:25:09.529 --> 00:25:12.360
for people with sensory disabilities
in particular.

00:25:12.702 --> 00:25:19.464
The AI-native interfaces are opening up
the ability to access all kinds of systems,

00:25:19.488 --> 00:25:21.666
new and old, new and legacy systems,

00:25:21.690 --> 00:25:23.523
and avoid the barriers

00:25:23.547 --> 00:25:27.798
that are so frequently inherent
within the user interfaces,

00:25:27.822 --> 00:25:30.121
like the websites and the
applications that we must use,

00:25:30.145 --> 00:25:33.450
that we traditionally have just
had to use to access those systems.

00:25:34.569 --> 00:25:36.704
Software development
is being democratized.

00:25:36.735 --> 00:25:40.777
Natural language has become the
universal programming language,

00:25:40.801 --> 00:25:44.132
and that means that anyone,
including people with disabilities,

00:25:44.156 --> 00:25:48.917
are finding it easier and easier to use
the accessible mainstream AI coding tools

00:25:49.318 --> 00:25:51.318
to solve their own access problems.

00:25:51.999 --> 00:25:56.665
And then when we build something,
we can share it legally as open source

00:25:57.225 --> 00:26:00.294
and collaborate on GitHub on
an accessible GitHub platform

00:26:00.318 --> 00:26:04.010
and build communities of
like-minded individuals

00:26:04.034 --> 00:26:09.191
to help maintain and improve the
accessible solutions that we're creating.

00:26:10.463 --> 00:26:13.896
Now, I wanna leave you
with a personal story,

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:18.522
a personal example of how all of this
recently came together for me

00:26:18.546 --> 00:26:19.556
over the last couple of months,

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:24.330
and it's really… it's this personal
experience that made all this click for me,

00:26:24.354 --> 00:26:31.159
that really took what I was observing
out in the world,

00:26:31.183 --> 00:26:33.715
in the disability community,
the technology community,

00:26:33.715 --> 00:26:38.478
and after having a kind of a sustained two
or three or four months worth of…

00:26:38.502 --> 00:26:42.212
It was probably about three months
worth of tense, sustained personal effort,

00:26:42.236 --> 00:26:44.333
I was like, "Oh, okay.
The game has changed here."

00:26:44.977 --> 00:26:47.274
All right? Let's dig into
this personal example.

00:26:49.307 --> 00:26:53.543
Back in March, I attended the CSUN
Assistive Technology Conference

00:26:53.567 --> 00:26:54.461
in Los Angeles.

00:26:54.485 --> 00:26:57.626
I live in Raleigh-Durham on the East
Coast of the United States.

00:26:57.626 --> 00:27:01.965
The CSUN Conference is on the West
Coast out in Anaheim, near Los Angeles.

00:27:01.965 --> 00:27:03.965
Well, it's a week-long conference.

00:27:04.528 --> 00:27:06.400
On the Saturday morning
after the conference,

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:11.141
I got up and made my way
to the Los Angeles International Airport,

00:27:12.036 --> 00:27:15.820
got to my plane,
sat down in my seat on the plane,

00:27:15.844 --> 00:27:18.548
and some people showed up beside me,

00:27:18.572 --> 00:27:22.549
and they refused to sit beside me
because of my seeing eye dog.

00:27:22.549 --> 00:27:26.214
I have a big German—a lovely German
shepherd seeing eye dog named Loki,

00:27:26.238 --> 00:27:29.690
who is absolutely wonderful and friendly,
and he was laying there quietly,

00:27:29.714 --> 00:27:32.089
but these people did
not wanna sit beside me.

00:27:32.241 --> 00:27:34.647
And it so happens that
a few rows behind me,

00:27:34.671 --> 00:27:39.052
there was a social media rock star,
named Jen Hamilton,

00:27:39.076 --> 00:27:42.094
and she started recording this interaction
and intervened.

00:27:42.444 --> 00:27:46.775
And we have a short clip from
Jen's original social media post here

00:27:46.799 --> 00:27:47.878
to share with you.

00:27:53.139 --> 00:27:54.160
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> This is not right

00:27:54.502 --> 00:27:56.932
<v Ed (in video)> There's something
called the Americans with Disabilities Act

00:27:56.956 --> 00:27:58.956
that allows us to have our service animal.

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:02.793
<v Another passenger (in video)> Yeah, we have a service animal too.

00:28:03.217 --> 00:28:05.431
<v Jen Hamilton (in video)> I mean I'll be happy
to sit with him.

00:28:05.708 --> 00:28:06.771
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> Are you?

00:28:06.795 --> 00:28:08.274
<v Jen (in video)> Yeah, I'd be happy to sit with him.

00:28:08.298 --> 00:28:10.316
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> Thank you.
<v Jen (in video)> Absolutely.

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:11.488
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> And are you sure, ma'am?

00:28:11.488 --> 00:28:12.744
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> I get to hang out with a dog.

00:28:12.768 --> 00:28:15.122
<v Jen (in video)> Oh, I mean,
that is an honor to sit over there.

00:28:15.146 --> 00:28:16.269
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> Thank you.

00:28:19.187 --> 00:28:20.946
<v Jen (in video)> Hi, my name is Jen. Can I sit with you?

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:21.858
<v Ed (in video)> Do you mind
if I just slide over?

00:28:21.858 --> 00:28:24.046
<v Jen (in video)> Oh, of course. Yeah.
<v Ed (in video)> Okay.

00:28:25.086 --> 00:28:27.020
<v Ed> That was Jen Hamilton that you heard.

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:30.714
She recorded that,
and she intervened with a very—

00:28:30.801 --> 00:28:34.878
just a wonderful moment of advocacy
for people with disabilities.

00:28:34.983 --> 00:28:39.366
And she recorded that video.
It's longer than that.

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:41.156
Actually, it might be
a two- or three-minute video,

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:42.222
the whole thing.

00:28:42.281 --> 00:28:46.241
And she published that a few hours
after we landed, and it went viral.

00:28:46.241 --> 00:28:48.250
And I published a follow-up video
of that video,

00:28:48.250 --> 00:28:50.557
and that went viral on social media.

00:28:50.557 --> 00:28:54.901
And then after a few days,
the story hopped over,

00:28:54.925 --> 00:28:57.893
crossed the chasm from social
media to mainstream media

00:28:57.917 --> 00:29:02.158
and was featured in just a wide
variety of mainstream news outlets.

00:29:02.185 --> 00:29:05.129
And it was just a beautiful moment

00:29:05.153 --> 00:29:08.680
where Jen and I really
just helped a lot of people,

00:29:08.704 --> 00:29:10.205
literally millions of people,

00:29:10.229 --> 00:29:15.054
understand what effective advocacy can
look like for people with disabilities.

00:29:15.873 --> 00:29:17.784
Now, what the videos did not show

00:29:18.265 --> 00:29:23.423
was the conversation that Jen and I had

00:29:23.447 --> 00:29:25.937
all the way from Los Angeles
back to Raleigh-Durham,

00:29:25.937 --> 00:29:27.136
which is a five-hour flight.

00:29:27.781 --> 00:29:30.331
And when Jen approached me,
I had no idea who she was.

00:29:30.331 --> 00:29:34.069
I did not know that she was such
an amazing social media rock star,

00:29:34.093 --> 00:29:36.320
and by the way,
a New York Times bestselling author.

00:29:37.144 --> 00:29:41.332
But Jen challenged me to start using video

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:44.520
to further my work,

00:29:45.130 --> 00:29:48.743
really my life's work of advocating
for people with disabilities.

00:29:49.624 --> 00:29:51.164
And I accepted her challenge,

00:29:52.044 --> 00:29:57.145
and quickly realized that
working with video as a blind person

00:29:57.557 --> 00:30:00.561
was gonna be very difficult
due to the limitations

00:30:01.148 --> 00:30:03.033
that existed within
the video editing tools.

00:30:03.486 --> 00:30:08.999
Being able to express myself
in the medium of video as a blind person

00:30:09.023 --> 00:30:11.835
was frankly impossible at the time.

00:30:12.827 --> 00:30:15.937
Of course, being the nerd
and the developer that I am,

00:30:15.961 --> 00:30:19.483
I turned to GitHub Copilot CLI
and some other tools.

00:30:19.549 --> 00:30:21.287
And let me walk you through what I did.

00:30:22.054 --> 00:30:24.781
Like I said,
I used the GitHub Copilot CLI.

00:30:25.097 --> 00:30:28.228
That's my go-to
kind of AI coding tool.

00:30:28.252 --> 00:30:29.434
And more than that, actually.

00:30:29.434 --> 00:30:32.084
It's really my go-to tool for just
about everything these days.

00:30:32.106 --> 00:30:37.727
And I started having a conversation about,
well, how can I solve this barrier?

00:30:37.751 --> 00:30:39.086
How can I overcome this barrier?

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:41.619
How can I create technology
that enables me as a blind person

00:30:42.133 --> 00:30:44.201
to really produce videos independently?

00:30:45.163 --> 00:30:48.632
And Copilot and I did many things.

00:30:48.634 --> 00:30:51.157
One, we built some custom skills,

00:30:51.679 --> 00:30:53.368
which I'll talk about in a minute.

00:30:53.539 --> 00:30:56.872
That was skills for Copilot
specifically that work with video.

00:30:57.135 --> 00:31:01.140
And Copilot helped me find
this open source tool called Whisper.

00:31:01.253 --> 00:31:02.534
It's from OpenAI.

00:31:02.554 --> 00:31:07.250
On the screen here it says openai/whisper,
and that's the name of the repo,

00:31:07.250 --> 00:31:09.429
the organization and the repo on GitHub.

00:31:09.429 --> 00:31:10.653
That's how that's written.

00:31:10.677 --> 00:31:13.376
And Whisper is a wonderful
open source tool

00:31:13.376 --> 00:31:16.698
that I'm using to convert all the speech

00:31:16.722 --> 00:31:20.030
in videos into text

00:31:20.438 --> 00:31:24.816
and to create transcripts of videos
and then also closed captions later.

00:31:25.795 --> 00:31:32.474
And then I make extensive use of an
amazing open source project called FFmpeg,

00:31:32.498 --> 00:31:34.768
which by the way is really…

00:31:34.792 --> 00:31:40.934
It's the project that drives all
modern video tools and video players

00:31:40.958 --> 00:31:42.763
that you might encounter.

00:31:43.346 --> 00:31:44.618
It's really an amazing project.

00:31:44.618 --> 00:31:46.520
If you haven't looked into FFmpeg,
check it out.

00:31:46.544 --> 00:31:47.568
Okay,

00:31:47.592 --> 00:31:49.510
but using these tools
and these building blocks,

00:31:50.169 --> 00:31:55.213
I created what I'm just calling
a blind-native video production toolbox.

00:31:56.702 --> 00:32:00.590
And it basically… Using this toolbox,
I can basically do these things.

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:02.856
I can understand the key
moments in videos.

00:32:02.856 --> 00:32:07.750
Now, when I go out and I'm recording
raw video content for a video,

00:32:07.750 --> 00:32:13.006
I might come home with 10, 15,
maybe 30 minutes worth of raw video,

00:32:13.030 --> 00:32:16.591
and I gotta understand that. I need
to understand what's happening visually

00:32:16.615 --> 00:32:22.832
inside of that footage so I can pull out
the 60 or 90 seconds that make the story

00:32:22.856 --> 00:32:24.234
that I really wanna tell.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:26.362
I can do that primarily using skills.

00:32:27.047 --> 00:32:29.713
I can arrange those key moments
into a story

00:32:29.737 --> 00:32:33.687
using some FFmpeg-based tools,
video editing tools.

00:32:34.224 --> 00:32:37.143
I can reorient videos
from landscape to portrait.

00:32:37.162 --> 00:32:39.162
Sometimes I use a GoPro camera.

00:32:39.675 --> 00:32:44.360
When I'm out in the wild, I mounted a GoPro
camera on my seeing eye dog's harness,

00:32:44.874 --> 00:32:47.585
and that shoots in landscape format,

00:32:47.609 --> 00:32:52.209
so I have to cut that down to portrait
orientation for mobile phones.

00:32:52.859 --> 00:32:55.537
I can add voiceovers
and mix that into the audio,

00:32:55.907 --> 00:32:58.009
and then I can burn
in captions before I publish,

00:32:58.033 --> 00:33:00.737
which of course is absolutely
required for accessibility.

00:33:00.786 --> 00:33:02.214
These are the things I can do now,

00:33:03.985 --> 00:33:05.588
I always have human review.

00:33:05.588 --> 00:33:08.269
Sighted human review
on the videos that I create,

00:33:08.293 --> 00:33:11.120
but I'm at the point where
I can create them independently

00:33:11.144 --> 00:33:13.006
and more often than not,

00:33:13.030 --> 00:33:17.557
my wife, who primarily provides
my sighted review of my videos,

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:20.199
gives a thumbs up and no further editing.

00:33:20.311 --> 00:33:24.978
Yeah, I wanted to share the first
video that I created with this toolbox

00:33:25.213 --> 00:33:27.466
that I recorded, edited, produced,

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:32.314
and published completely 100%
independently as a blind person.

00:33:38.552 --> 00:33:40.195
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> Yeah, yeah.

00:33:40.219 --> 00:33:41.219
<v Ed (in video)> Forward.

00:33:42.895 --> 00:33:46.182
<v Ed (voiceover in video)> Crossing the street is the most dangerous
thing that Loki and I do together.

00:33:46.206 --> 00:33:48.358
His job is to stop at the down curbs,

00:33:48.382 --> 00:33:52.233
and my job is to decide
when it's safe to cross the street.

00:33:53.915 --> 00:33:55.202
<v Ed (in video)> Left

00:33:55.999 --> 00:33:57.616
<v Ed (voiceover in video)> This is where Loki really shines.

00:33:57.616 --> 00:34:00.283
It's a crowded sidewalk
with lots of obstacles.

00:34:00.711 --> 00:34:02.378
We're approaching a family ahead.

00:34:02.382 --> 00:34:07.249
Watch how he guides me around the family
and avoids the open car door to the left.

00:34:17.951 --> 00:34:21.567
Now we're approaching dense pedestrian
traffic that Loki must negotiate

00:34:21.602 --> 00:34:22.985
as we enter the subway station.

00:34:23.783 --> 00:34:26.051
Watch how he stops
at the top of the stairs

00:34:26.284 --> 00:34:29.037
and waits for me to give
the command to proceed.

00:34:29.285 --> 00:34:32.618
Just like down curbs,
that's super important for our safety.

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:37.103
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> Yeah, right here.

00:34:37.127 --> 00:34:39.696
<v Ed (in video)> Right, right.

00:34:40.211 --> 00:34:41.255
Here?

00:34:46.578 --> 00:34:48.866
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> Right, and then there's
some stairs on the right

00:34:52.088 --> 00:34:53.173
<v Ed (in video)> Okay. Stairs down?

00:34:53.245 --> 00:34:54.300
<v Unidentified speaker (in video)> Down the stairs, yeah.

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:55.300
<v Ed (in video)> Forward.

00:34:55.300 --> 00:34:56.500
Steady, boy.

00:34:57.753 --> 00:35:02.031
[music getting louder]

00:35:12.895 --> 00:35:17.165
<v Ed> Well, thank you so much for hosting me
here at WordPress Accessibility Day.

00:35:17.165 --> 00:35:20.367
It's really been a true pleasure to share

00:35:20.391 --> 00:35:23.586
this exciting renaissance of achievement
for people with disabilities.

00:35:23.587 --> 00:35:25.429
I think it's just getting started,

00:35:25.453 --> 00:35:29.928
and I'm just so, so incredibly thankful
to be a part of it

00:35:29.952 --> 00:35:34.205
and to hopefully… and to frankly,
hopefully move it along a little bit

00:35:34.229 --> 00:35:37.587
and empower people
with disabilities to build technology

00:35:37.636 --> 00:35:39.303
and use technology more effectively.

00:35:39.881 --> 00:35:45.597
I am—my handle on most
of the socials is edsummersnc.

00:35:45.707 --> 00:35:49.645
E-D-S-U-M-M-E-R-S-N-C.

00:35:50.237 --> 00:35:54.973
And particularly my best place to connect
or follow me is probably LinkedIn

00:35:55.389 --> 00:35:59.545
and then Instagram and TikTok
as well for primarily video content.

00:36:00.237 --> 00:36:03.002
Hope you'll follow me
or connect with me there.

00:36:03.002 --> 00:36:04.891
And then I think we're wrapping up.
Yeah.

00:36:04.915 --> 00:36:05.951
Thank you so much.

00:36:05.951 --> 00:36:07.618
I really appreciate the opportunity.

00:36:15.364 --> 00:36:17.707
<v Sherose> Hi, Ed. Thank you so much for joining us.

00:36:19.148 --> 00:36:20.964
<v Ed> Hello, Sherose. Is that Sherose?

00:36:20.964 --> 00:36:22.463
<v Sherose> It's Sherose. How are you doing?

00:36:22.463 --> 00:36:24.316
<v Ed> I'm great. Thank—can you hear me?

00:36:24.316 --> 00:36:27.530
<v Sherose> I think you have
a lot of fans in the chat

00:36:27.554 --> 00:36:29.492
for yourself, but probably mostly Loki.

00:36:29.516 --> 00:36:31.293
He seems like such a good boy.

00:36:31.293 --> 00:36:33.493
<v Ed> Oh, yeah.
Yeah, he's the real rock star.

00:36:33.517 --> 00:36:37.358
Yeah, he's totally the star of the show,
and rightfully so.

00:36:38.134 --> 00:36:39.929
<v Sherose> We have a few questions in the Q&amp;A.

00:36:39.929 --> 00:36:41.130
I wanna remind the audience

00:36:41.614 --> 00:36:44.276
they're welcome to add more,
but we only have a few minutes,

00:36:44.300 --> 00:36:48.691
and I think what I'm gonna do is give you
a loaded combination question. You ready?

00:36:48.691 --> 00:36:50.551
<v Ed> Oh, boy. Okay. All right, let's go.

00:36:50.754 --> 00:36:51.892
<v Sherose> Someone asks,

00:36:51.916 --> 00:36:56.533
"Could you describe a typical day in the
life of the accessibility unit at GitHub?"

00:36:56.557 --> 00:36:59.154
And I think it'd be helpful
if you touch on

00:36:59.178 --> 00:37:05.564
perhaps how developers evaluate AI-native
or AI-integrated products.

00:37:07.168 --> 00:37:08.910
<v Ed> Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:37:09.563 --> 00:37:12.054
Typical day in the life. Well…

00:37:14.106 --> 00:37:20.353
Yeah, so we really started investing
in accessibility in a structured way

00:37:20.377 --> 00:37:22.178
about five years ago at GitHub.

00:37:22.226 --> 00:37:27.484
And about a year into that, the leadership
team created my position,

00:37:27.508 --> 00:37:29.360
which is the head of accessibility,

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:33.475
to basically accelerate the investment

00:37:33.499 --> 00:37:35.513
and guide the investment
from an executive level.

00:37:35.938 --> 00:37:39.500
I was incredibly blessed to be
recruited into this position,

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:42.596
and I've been here for a little
over four years now.

00:37:42.596 --> 00:37:45.106
And throughout this whole journey,

00:37:45.130 --> 00:37:47.491
we spent the first,
what, three or so years,

00:37:47.515 --> 00:37:50.554
three or four years, really
focused on those internal systems

00:37:50.977 --> 00:37:55.923
the processes that are required in order
to integrate accessibility throughout

00:37:56.261 --> 00:37:59.026
not only the product development process,
but throughout the company.

00:37:59.026 --> 00:38:02.886
You know, for our events
and our social media,

00:38:02.910 --> 00:38:06.346
our comms, you know, our workplace,
all that kind of stuff.

00:38:06.387 --> 00:38:11.384
And, you know, thanks to not only
our accessibility team at GitHub,

00:38:11.408 --> 00:38:12.586
who are amazing,

00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:15.167
but also the employees at GitHub.

00:38:15.394 --> 00:38:18.840
We call them Hubbers, is our kind
of nickname for GitHub employees.

00:38:20.489 --> 00:38:22.644
They're amazing. I mean,
they've just been amazing.

00:38:23.159 --> 00:38:26.343
I've been building and leading
accessibility teams now for 15 or 16 years,

00:38:26.367 --> 00:38:27.873
about 16 years now.

00:38:28.846 --> 00:38:36.302
I've, in the past, described accessibility
as a process of a Sisyphean task.

00:38:36.347 --> 00:38:40.828
We're referring to Sisyphus, who had
to push the rock up the hill every day,

00:38:40.852 --> 00:38:43.154
and, at night, it rolled
back down the hill,

00:38:43.178 --> 00:38:46.091
and then he had to do it again,
same thing again the next day.

00:38:46.091 --> 00:38:51.289
But what has happened at GitHub is
I don't push the rock up the hill.

00:38:51.289 --> 00:38:55.490
I run downhill trying to keep up with the
rock because it has so much momentum

00:38:55.514 --> 00:38:58.744
because of this wonderful
team of people that we work with here.

00:39:00.405 --> 00:39:02.297
We're at the point where,
you know, we have

00:39:02.321 --> 00:39:05.104
accessibility largely
integrated into our processes.

00:39:05.204 --> 00:39:12.258
And it's withstanding the test of time

00:39:12.282 --> 00:39:18.520
with rocket-fueled
growth of our user base.

00:39:18.876 --> 00:39:21.753
GitHub is up to over
180 million developers now.

00:39:21.753 --> 00:39:23.956
When I came,
I think it was about 50 million.

00:39:24.879 --> 00:39:26.042
Lots of growth there.

00:39:26.066 --> 00:39:29.097
The growth of AI, which has
even been incredibly competitive.

00:39:29.097 --> 00:39:32.612
And throughout this
we've been able to maintain accessibility

00:39:32.636 --> 00:39:36.264
as a primary,
as an engineering fundamental at GitHub.

00:39:36.278 --> 00:39:38.114
So, it's been really amazing.

00:39:38.155 --> 00:39:42.205
And over the last year
or so we've revamped our strategy,

00:39:42.229 --> 00:39:46.841
and we've tried to build on that success,
to build on that foundation,

00:39:46.865 --> 00:39:48.120
and to…

00:39:49.113 --> 00:39:52.948
And we've added a couple
additional strategic priorities

00:39:52.972 --> 00:39:55.443
for empowering our customers
that are building on GitHub

00:39:55.467 --> 00:39:57.315
to be able
to meet their accessibility goals.

00:39:57.768 --> 00:40:00.479
For example, we just recently…
Well, not just recently,

00:40:00.503 --> 00:40:02.419
maybe about this time last year,

00:40:02.921 --> 00:40:07.190
we released a free
and open source accessibility scanner.

00:40:07.579 --> 00:40:10.771
You can find that at
github/accessibility-scanner

00:40:11.351 --> 00:40:15.725
which we are continuing to support and grow.
And the adoption's been really well.

00:40:15.725 --> 00:40:18.695
It's an example of some tools
that we were using internally

00:40:18.719 --> 00:40:22.695
that we've refactored and open sourced
and have made available for the community.

00:40:23.535 --> 00:40:25.354
Lots of great growth there

00:40:25.378 --> 00:40:29.778
towards enabling those of you
out there who are building on GitHub

00:40:29.802 --> 00:40:34.234
to meet your accessibility goals
and kind of leverage best practices

00:40:34.258 --> 00:40:37.032
of automation and AI and whatnot.

00:40:37.705 --> 00:40:40.947
And then another initiative we created is

00:40:41.598 --> 00:40:43.560
to help improve
the accessibility of open source.

00:40:44.140 --> 00:40:46.033
The world runs on open source.

00:40:46.033 --> 00:40:51.425
Open source is built on GitHub
by mostly unpaid volunteers.

00:40:51.458 --> 00:40:52.910
I'm sure many of you out there…

00:40:52.934 --> 00:40:54.430
Well, WordPress, for example,

00:40:54.430 --> 00:40:55.915
is a great example of that.

00:40:56.741 --> 00:40:58.408
<v Sherose> It sure is. Yeah.

00:40:58.764 --> 00:41:00.093
Ed, thank you so much.

00:41:00.093 --> 00:41:01.268
We are out of time.

00:41:01.268 --> 00:41:05.601
This conversation
can continue in the chat.

00:41:05.893 --> 00:41:08.897
I wanna go ahead and thank everyone
for attending this session with Ed.

00:41:10.554 --> 00:41:19.477
You can also connect on social media using
#WPA11yDay and #WPAD2026.

00:41:20.023 --> 00:41:28.519
I would also appreciate it if you go
to wpaccessibility.day/2026/feedback.